# Reviewed exceptions to `cargo xtask net-ban`. No exception is expected for
# Bytescape's own code: it never opens a network connection.
#
# The one anticipated kind is the local connection to the display server
# (Wayland or X11) that the windowing libraries make. It is allowed in the
# interface only, and every entry names the windowing crate or crates that
# pull the item in. An entry holds only while EVERY path to the item in the
# dependency graph goes through one of those crates. It never applies to
# anything in the dependency closure of `bytescape-helper`.
#
# The second kind is the Linux screen-reader adapter. `accesskit_unix` talks
# AT-SPI over local D-Bus connections through `zbus` and `async-io`. These are
# admitted only while every path to them goes through `accesskit_unix`, only
# for Linux builds of the interface, and never in the helper. The condition is
# that EVERY address the adapter connects to uses exactly the `unix` transport
# (each `;`-separated entry; `unixexec`, `tcp`, `nonce-tcp`, `autolaunch` and
# `launchd` are refused): the session bus (`DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`, or unset
# with `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus`), `AT_SPI_BUS_ADDRESS`, and the reply of
# `org.a11y.Bus.GetAddress`. The interface checks the AT-SPI address before it
# creates the adapter and pins the checked address for the process, or runs
# without the adapter. The library itself would also accept `tcp:` and
# `unixexec:` addresses, so this check in the interface is what keeps the
# adapter local.
#
# Every other socket, HTTP, TLS and DNS crate on the ban list can never be
# allowed here, whatever `via` an entry names (see `never_allowed` in
# xtask/src/net_ban.rs).
#
# Entry formats, one per line, with the reason after `#`:
#
#   crate NAME via CRATE[,CRATE...]
#   feature CRATE/FEATURE via CRATE[,CRATE...]
#   source PATH
#
# `source PATH` allows one Rust source file (path relative to the repository
# root) to mention a networking API; it needs a reason like the others.
#
# Example (not active):
#
#   feature rustix/net via winit,softbuffer  # Wayland and X11 socket to the local display server
#
# Every added line also needs a maintainer's explicit approval.

feature rustix/net via winit,softbuffer,accesskit_unix  # display server socket; session bus socket for screen readers
crate zbus via accesskit_unix  # D-Bus client for the local session bus (screen readers)
crate async-io via accesskit_unix  # event loop under zbus; local sockets only
